e invasive your technology or the  larger the claim you have the more data  you will need to be able to prove to  people like the fda  that  your technology is safe and providing an  accurate diagnosis or screening  another thing we really want to think  about is designing with equity in mind  and  for me i really like to use the world  health organizations  definition for them equity is the  absence of unfair avoidable or remedial  differences among groups of people  whether those groups are defined  socially economically demographically or  geographically  or by other dimensions of inequality  health is a fundamental human right  and health equity is achieved when  everyone can attain their full potential  for health and well-being  i think that this definition is great  because it's really expansive in terms  of what we think about when we're  thinking about differences but it also  is very cognizant of the fact that  we  are different in general as people  um when we think about health equity in  designing new medical devices  for us we really tried to think about  diagnostics in terms of stuff that's too  expensive  um getting a diagnostic that might take  too long or it's too far away for you to  get to  it might take too many steps so there  are too many follow-ups for you to deal  with  and also trying to combat stigma of  diagnosis  how he did that was through a variety of  things and  they really kind of point to these three  critical  aspects of health equity we want to try  and increase access both physically by  making sure our device  is battery operated and portable but we  also want to make it accessible  economically so we're trying to make  sure our tests are less than three  dollars  we want to increase access by  eliminating the need for follow-ups so  we want to make it one step and because  we are able to have the room connected  to telehealth  it will  hopefully allow  for  easier and faster diagnosis in  places that may or may not  have  health clinics  again we also want to design  with stigma reduction in mind because we  recognize that  being screened positive for something  even like covet 19 comes with  social  things  a part of the beauty of the roomie is  that it is small and discreet and that a  lot of the screenings or diagnostics can  be folded into regular health checkups  we also believe that the oral  administration of the rumi will help  decrease stigma by not leaving any signs  of testing left over  we also believe that  we want to increase equity in point of  point of care diagnostics because  accuracy is critical for the continuum  of care and the development of future  health plans  and we believe we can do that  by eliminating a lab and reducing human  error and providing a qualitative and  quantitative results  almost immediately allowing providers to  understand  not only if their patient is positive or  negative but also hopefully where they  are in  their disease spectrum  granted there are always unintended  consequences when you're developing new  technologies and when it comes to point  of care diagnostics it's really critical  for all of us to think about  what is our role in the continuum of  care like what what happens after  your device tells someone they are  screened positive  is it  do you need to provide information on  treatment do you need to ensure that  these results get to a particular  body such as the public health  department  a lot of these questions are not  necessarily things that we will think  about very early on in the design  process because  it takes so much  just to get your technology to people  however  things like unintended hipaa privacy  violations will come into play when  you're trying to get fda approval so  it's always good to think about it  early on another really important aspect  of innovation is ensuring that the  technology we develop does not increase  inequities that currently exist  and one of the things that  i think we've really seen recently is  this reliance on telehealth  especially because of coca-19  however  telehealth requires people to not only  have an individual device but for that  device to be able to conduct a hipaa  compliance appointment which requires  strong and consistent internet you also  need a room or space where you can talk  to your provider  alone which  is not really possible for a large  proportion of this country let alone the  world so when we're developing  technologies it's always important to  remember  that not everything can solve social  problems so we need to be cognizant of  those social problems as we move forward  so thank you guys so much for listening  to us i just want to go over a little  bit of where we at steradian  technologies are now  we are the grand prize winners of  x prizes 6 million rapid coca-19 tested  testing open innovation track  competition  this has allowed us to begin our initial  feasibility study  where we are doing device integration  which means we are integrating the roomy  handheld device  and the  disposable biological cartridge  we're also conducting a feasibility  study which is our first study  with human subjects using the root me  and we are developing a regulatory  pathway with  different consultants and regulatory  scientists  we are also  because we are able to work as a  platform detection device  working on  detecting early stage lung cancer with  johnson and johnson  and developing a multiplex with  kova 19 and h1n1 and hopefully in the  future  the entire flu spectrum  with la jolla institute for immunology  and x prize and we also are having very  interesting and exciting conversations  about using the roomies  breath concentration technology in tv  so we're super super excited about all  this stuff that's to come  we hope that you guys enjoyed our  presentation and we're able to learn  about these different things in terms of  what it takes to go from ideation to  innovation  how you can design with people and  equity in mind  and what are the advantages of point of  care diagnostics  lastly we want to say thank you to  everyone  on our team everyone at lab roots  everyone at xprize and of course our  incredible scientists and innovators  thanks  and thank you both for that informative  presentation  we'd like to now start the live q a  portion of the webinar and if you have  any questions you want to ask please do  so now just click on ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  so let's get started it looks like we  already have some great questions coming  in  our first question how would  multiplexing work  sure i can take that question this is  asthma  um so the way that multiplexing would  work is on the device side we would need  to have  different filters that could filter out  different wavelengths and then  be able to bring them to the  photomultiplier tube  and be able to differentiate which  wavelengths are coming in because we  would still want to know  the relative concentration of each one  of those biomarkers or pathogens that  we're looking at and then on the  biological side what we would need to do  is have different molecular beacons with  different fluorophores attached to them  to be able to differentiate and then it  would be very important to make sure  that there isn't any cross reactivity in  the biosensor itself so it would  definitely be a multi-step process  but it can it can be done which is very  exciting  thank you so much and how would you  expand into other samples  um so  when we think of other samples on the  device side not much would change  what would likely change is on the  cartridge side  so if we are looking at blood  when you take blood when you take a  blood sample  you may have to isolate it into its  different portions and then pipe that  out  the layer that you're looking at  insert that into the roomy device and  then look at it so that's just a very  high level way of of thinking about it  but  you know as innovators and as scientists  we definitely believe that there's  something there that we haven't hacked  yet um but i think expanding into other  samples just strictly from a  testing life cycle stage  we would have to do a lot of lab testing  um without even using blood in the in  the beginning we would just have to  figure out different ways that we can  isolate things so  it would be it was again a multi-step  process all of these would be multi-step  processes  thank you so much esma and i want to  thank our audience for these great  questions  where do you see your device being  adopted in screening or diagnostics  yeah  okay so it really depends on  what disease we will be looking at i  think for  things like lung cancer we're really  looking at screening  in terms of  covenanting at home use it would also be  screening but covid19 in the clinic  would be a diagnosis  so  use case also really matters here in  terms of where it would be adopted but  we do believe that  because it's  a platform device it would be able to  sit well in any  primary healthcare  position  wonderful thank you so much and we have  a couple more questions coming in  was your pivot intentional and how early  in the development was your original  device  um that's a great question so the pivot  was not intentional um  we were  very very excited to kind of be in the  space of deep tech and  the recreation of human eyesight to just  human supersite  would help for anything from retinal  degeneration to better accuracy and  surgery  and so we had started our pre-clinical  testing and studies for that but when  the pandemic hit we have to pull back  all of that because nobody was allowed  to go into the hospitals around  you know march 2020  everything had to seize and halt um and  we were just very serendipitously  talking in a meeting and  we were discussing the lack of public  health initiative when it came to rapid  diagnostics and  we kept thinking why aren't they fast  enough and as we dug through that  question  it dawned upon us that  hold up what if we use our device  to  maybe use photonics for diagnostic  testing and that's that's how it came up  so  we we didn't even have a device we just  had the internal mechanisms of eyesight  recreation and we started from  you know  a blank slate  so i would say we weren't even early in  development this was  nascent  thank you so much  we have one more question coming in is  there a difference between communicable  and non-communicable disease  diseases  yeah i can answer that one there is a  difference for us in terms of how and  what we are going to identify using the  rumi  for communicable and non-communicable  diseases  i think  the  biggest differentiator is that with  communicable diseases we're  more than likely able to look for  the disease itself whereas with  non-communicable diseases we're looking  for  the way that the disease will affect the  body if that makes sense  absolutely  ladies i want to thank you for your  presentation today would you like to  provide any closing remarks before we go  yes um we are so grateful to be here and  um to engage with a community that  innovates and tries to make the world a  better place through science and we're  always looking for  scientists who are interested in  technology such as ours we love  collaborating um so please feel free to  reach out to us  and try  do you want to add anything  no i think usma totally got it all to  everyone  perfect well thank you again asantra for  your time today and for your important  research and before we go i want to  thank our audience for joining us today  and for their interesting questions and  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted on the on-demand  period will be addressed via the speaker  using your contact information you  provided the time of registration and  this webcast can be viewed on demand for  two years until november 2023 labrets  will alert you via email when it's  available for replay we encourage you to  share that email with your colleagues  who may have missed the live event until  next time take care everyone and stay  safe bye
 

